            "\\section{Discussion}\n",
            "\\label{sec:discussion}\n",
            "\n",
            "Potential points of discussion:\n",
            "\n",
            "\\begin{itemize}\n",
            "    \\item Figure~\\ref{fig:fewrel-limit-examples} shows that, given a limited budget\n",
            "    of annotations, it is better to have a diverse set of relation types than large numbers\n",
            "    of relations per type.\n",
            "    \n",
            "    \\item Gap between the few-shot relation matching and the relation extraction tasks.\n",
            "    Looking at the confusion matrix, it seems that the the most common error is flipping\n",
            "    between \"no relation\" and the correct relation (or vice-versa). Modeling the\n",
            "    \\textsc{NIL} properly is likely to yield gains.\n",
            "    \n",
            "    \\item We have not tried to increase, beyond two, the number of co-occuring entities to\n",
            "    define statement equivalence.\n",
            "    \\item This work requires a entity linking annotations. It would be interesting to relax\n",
            "    this requirement so that NER + heuristics based linking may be sufficient even if the result\n",
            "    is noisy,  \\textit{particularly if we require two entity mentions to co-occur within a \n",
            "    window of text}. (i.e., is mention ambiguity still a problem when considering \"2-gram\"\n",
            "    entities?).\n",
            "    \\item \n",
            "\\end{itemize}\n",
            "\n",
            "\\section{Conclusion and Future Work}\n",
            "\n",
            "In this paper we study the problem of producing useful relation representations directly\n",
            "from text. We describe a novel training setup, which we call \\textit{matching the blanks},\n",
            "which relies solely on entity resolution annotations. When coupled with a new architecture for \n",
            "fine-tuning relation representations in BERT, our models achieves state-of-the-art results on\n",
            "three  relation extraction tasks, and outperforms human accuracy on few-shot relation matching.\n",
            "In addition, we show how the new model is particularly effective\n",
            "in low-resource regimes, and we argue that it could significantly reduce the amount of human effort required to create relation extractors.\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "In future work, we plan to work on \\emph{relation discovery} by clustering relation \n",
            "statements that have similar representations according to \\mtbmodel.\n",
            "This would take us some of the way toward our goal of truly general purpose relation identification and extraction.\n",
            "We will also study representations of relations and entities that can be used to store relation triples in a distributed knowledge base. \n",
            "This is inspired by recent work in knowledge base embedding \\cite{bordes2013translating,nickel2016holographic}. \n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\\begin{comment}\n",
            "In this paper we study the problem of producing useful relation representations directly\n",
            "from text. We propose and investigate a range of architectures for relation encoding \n",
            "derived from the BERT, a deep Transformer model, producing an architecture (\\bertem{}) that \n",
            "achieves state-of-the-art performance on three relation extraction and one few-shot\n",
            "relation matching tasks.  We then propose a novel unsupervised training objective,\n",
            "which we call ``matching the blanks'', relying on automatically generated annotations\n",
            "from an off-the-shelf entity linking system. The intuition for matching the blanks\n",
            "is largely the same as Harris' distributional hypothesis: relation statements that contain\n",
            "the same set of entities are more likely to be semantically related than other\n",
            "relation statements.\n",
            "\n",
            "To evaluate our hypothesis, we train a \\bertem{} model combining the matching the\n",
            "blanks with the original masked language model task. We show that this new model\n",
            "can improve performance on all evaluated tasks beyond \\bertem{}. The new model also outperforms human accuracy on the few-shot relation matching task. Matching the blanks\n",
            "training shows to be particularly effective in boosting performance in low-resource cases.\n",
            "\\end{comment}\n",
